I've inspected the new navigation logic and cross-checked it against the analyzer. One verified correctness defect in the deferred-resolution pass, plus one test-correctness concern.

FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1582 — The source-order "deferred pass" rebinds an earlier *builtin* use to a user declaration that appears later. For `say abs\nset abs to 1\n`, the walk leaves the `abs` on line 1 unresolved (no scope entry yet, `src/lsp.rs:1526-1544`), which is correct: `analyzer.rs:216-220` says a builtin answers until the program "claims the name", and `first-set`/function declarations do not pre-claim it (only `constant`/`import` do, `analyzer.rs:223-229`). The deferred pass then sees `token_binding[index]==None`, walks `scope_decls` and assigns the line-2 `set abs` binding (`src/lsp.rs:1600-1613`), so `goto_definition(source,1,5)` answers the variable at 2:5 instead of `DefinitionKind::Builtin`, and `find_references` joins the builtin use to the variable. This contradicts the phase's stated "follows the analyzer and runtime" rule. Fix: in the deferred pass, when `is_builtin_name(name)`, only bind to a declaration that precedes the use or is a `Constant`/`Import`; otherwise leave the token unbound so the builtin fallback runs. VERIFIED: traced the exact path — walk leaves line-1 `abs` at `token_binding=None` (loop at 1527-1543 finds no scope entry), then 1582-1616 unconditionally re-resolves it via `scope_decls` to the line-2 `set` binding; no test pins the builtin-shadowed-by-later-`set` case.

2. [MINOR] tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:2504 — This test asserts that a bare `name` read inside a method body (`give back name`) resolves to the object's `has name` field, and `src/lsp.rs:1540` implements that by leaving object-scope field entries visible through the method's parent scope. Field access elsewhere in the suite is written `this.name` (e.g. line 2398), so if the analyzer does not bind object fields as bare lexical names inside method bodies, this test pins navigation behaviour the language rejects ("navigation models the analyzer" is the phase's claim). I could not run the analyzer to confirm, so this is deliberately marked MINOR. What would settle it: analyze/run the fixture `object Row\n  has name default "field"\n  to can name()\n    give back name\n  end\nend` and check whether `name` is an `Unknown variable` error; if it is, change the fixture/navigation to use `this.name` and drop the bare-field preference.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 2
